--- field object.crypto-algorithm.id
type: str

The crypto policy entry a process used, as an algorithm and an operation.

The identifier is the one exceptions name in `Algorithms` under
`Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions`, such as `md5.hmac` or
`sha1.digest`: an algorithm, a dot, then the operation it was used for.
`/usr/share/peios-crypto/algorithms.tsv` lists every entry libpcrypto
grades, with its grade and whether FIPS 140-3 approves it.

--- field object.crypto-algorithm.grade
type: str.enum
values: U | D | C | B | A
closed: true

The entry's grade on Peios's ladder, from U, practically broken, to A.

- `U`: practically broken, or forbidden by current standards, such as
  MD5's collision resistance.
- `D`: no practical break, but under 112-bit security or formally
  deprecated.
- `C`: 112-bit security.
- `B`: 128-bit security.
- `A`: 192-bit security or more.

Absent for an entry on the floor, which nothing can use, so no record
names one.

--- field object.crypto-exception.name
type: str

A crypto policy exception, by its key's name, `Owner.Purpose`, such as
`Radius.PacketAuth`.

The exception is the key of that name under
`Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions`.

--- field object.crypto-policy.reject-below
type: str.enum
values: U | D | C | B | A
closed: true

The grade below which the published policy refuses an entry.

At `U` only the floor is refused.

--- field object.crypto-policy.warn-below
type: str.enum
values: U | D | C | B | A
closed: true

The grade below which the published policy allows an entry but records
its use.

--- field object.crypto-policy.reject-non-fips
type: bool

Whether the published policy refuses every entry FIPS 140-3 doesn't approve.

The machine still doesn't operate in a FIPS-approved mode, because
libpcrypto's module isn't validated.

--- field object.crypto-policy.warn-non-fips
type: bool

Whether the published policy records the use of every entry FIPS 140-3
doesn't approve.

--- field object.crypto-policy.exceptions-may-reject
type: bool

Whether the published policy's exceptions may allow entries it would
otherwise refuse.

When false, an exception only stops an entry's use being warned about.

--- field object.crypto-policy.exception-count
type: uint

The number of exceptions in the published policy.

Exceptions cryptod dropped as invalid aren't counted.

--- field object.crypto-policy.defaults
type: bool

Whether the published policy is Peios's defaults, because
`Machine\System\Cryptography` doesn't exist.

--- event cryptod.policy.applied
tier: standard
gating: every policy cryptod publishes

cryptod compiled `Machine\System\Cryptography` and published the policy
libpcrypto reads.

cryptod compiles the key when it starts, and again whenever the key has
been still for a moment after a change. A compile that gives the policy
already published doesn't publish it again, and writes no record.

A published policy applies to processes started from then on. A process
reads the policy once, at its first use of libpcrypto, and keeps it, so a
running service keeps the policy it started with until it is restarted.

field: object.crypto-policy.reject-below           required
field: object.crypto-policy.warn-below             required
field: object.crypto-policy.reject-non-fips        required
field: object.crypto-policy.warn-non-fips          required
field: object.crypto-policy.exceptions-may-reject  required
field: object.crypto-policy.exception-count        required
field: object.crypto-policy.defaults               required
field: subject.token.sid                    optional
  cryptod's own user. Absent only if cryptod couldn't read its own token.

--- event cryptod.policy.failed
tier: essential
gating: none: every failure is recorded

A crypto policy couldn't be compiled, published or used as written.

There are three kinds of failure:

- **The compile failed.** A setting under `Machine\System\Cryptography`
  is present but can't be read, the Registry couldn't be read, or the
  policy is too large. cryptod keeps the policy it last published,
  because falling back to the defaults could loosen a policy that an
  administrator tightened. Fix the setting `outcome.detail` names.
- **An exception was dropped.** It can't apply as written, so it allows
  nothing, and the rest of the policy is published without it. Fix the
  exception `object.crypto-exception.name` names. It's recorded when
  first dropped, and when cryptod starts.
- **A process couldn't use the policy.** It fell back to the next copy,
  or to Peios's defaults, and reported why. `subject.process.pid` names
  it.

field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: registry-unreadable | invalid-setting | too-large | invalid-exception-name | unknown-algorithm | floor-algorithm | no-algorithms | invalid-algorithms | invalid-program-security | write-failed | copy-unreadable | copy-insecure | copy-malformed | copy-version | token-unreadable
  closed: false
  What failed.

  - `registry-unreadable`: cryptod couldn't read the key, as when it
    starts while the boot is still applying registry seeds. It tries
    again, after a second and then twice as long each time, at most a
    minute, and records the failure once until it passes.
  - `invalid-setting`: a value is present but has the wrong type or an
    unknown grade.
  - `too-large`: the policy, or one exception, doesn't fit the policy
    file.
  - `invalid-exception-name`: an exception's name isn't `Owner.Purpose`
    in printable ASCII.
  - `unknown-algorithm`: an exception names an entry libpcrypto doesn't
    grade, often a typing mistake.
  - `floor-algorithm`: an exception names an entry on the floor, which
    nothing can allow.
  - `no-algorithms`: an exception names no entries.
  - `invalid-algorithms`: an exception's `Algorithms` isn't a
    REG_MULTI_SZ.
  - `invalid-program-security`: an exception's `ProgramSecurity` is
    absent, isn't a security descriptor, or has no owner or no DACL.
  - `write-failed`: cryptod couldn't write a copy of the policy.
  - `copy-unreadable`: a process couldn't read a copy.
  - `copy-insecure`: a process refused a copy that someone other than
    SYSTEM or cryptod owns or could change.
  - `copy-malformed`: a process refused a copy that isn't a policy.
  - `copy-version`: a process refused a copy of a format version it
    doesn't know, which an older libpcrypto does after an update.
  - `token-unreadable`: a process couldn't read its own token, so it
    used no exceptions.
field: outcome.detail                       optional
field: object.crypto-exception.name         optional
  The exception dropped. Present when an exception, rather than the whole
  policy, failed.
field: subject.token.sid                    optional
  For a process's report, the user of its token. Otherwise cryptod's own
  user, absent only if cryptod couldn't read its own token.
field: subject.process.pid                  optional
  The process that reported it. Absent when cryptod failed.

--- event cryptod.algorithm.warned
tier: standard
gating: once per process for each entry, at its first use

A process used a crypto policy entry that the policy allows but warns
about.

libpcrypto reports the first use of each such entry in each process, and
cryptod records it. A forked child reports again. An entry's use under
an exception that doesn't warn is recorded as `cryptod.exception.used`
instead.

Before raising `Reject\MinimumGrade`, search these records to find the
programs that would stop working. A refused low-level digest ends the
program that asked for it.

The process is named by the token and pid the kernel recorded when it
connected to cryptod. A thread that was impersonating at that moment is
recorded as the user it impersonated. The pid still names the process.

field: object.crypto-algorithm.id           required
field: object.crypto-algorithm.grade        required
field: subject.token.sid                    required
  The user of the process's token.
field: subject.process.pid                  optional
  The process. Absent if the kernel recorded none.

--- event cryptod.exception.used
tier: essential
gating: once per process for each exception, at its first use

A process used a crypto policy exception.

libpcrypto reports each exception's first use in each process, whether
the exception warns or not, and cryptod records it, so an audit can always
find the weak crypto in use. A forked child reports again. The entry is
the first one the process used under the exception.

The process is named as in `cryptod.algorithm.warned`.

field: object.crypto-exception.name         required
field: object.crypto-algorithm.id           required
field: object.crypto-algorithm.grade        required
field: subject.token.sid                    required
  The user of the process's token.
field: subject.process.pid                  optional
  The process. Absent if the kernel recorded none.
